@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix pmw:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/works/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the Greek magical papyri
#
# Opened at the one piece the register itself singled out: the ascent ritual at
# PGM IV.475-829. It is the first text in this graph's ascent material that
# prescribes what to do with the body, states how often to repeat it, and lists
# in order what the operator will see if it is working. It is also the first
# that carries a vowel chain in full rather than as a picture or an ellipsis.
# The tradition instance was registered in traditions/registry-mediterranean.ttl
# and is moved here now that passages have been read.
#

pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Greek and demotic magical papyri"@en, "Griechische und demotische Zauberpapyri"@de ;
    skos:definition "The working handbooks of late antique Egypt: procedures with materials, hours, purity requirements, spoken formulae and stated signs of success."@en ;
    skos:note "What was read: the ascent ritual at PGM IV.475-829, in the translation of G. R. S. Mead, A Mithriac Ritual (Echoes from the Gnosis VI, London and Benares 1907), public domain. Nothing else of the corpus is opened, and this is a single piece out of a codex of thirty-six pages."@en ;
    skos:note "Two properties of the translation first used bear on the nodes that cite it, and Mead states both himself. He removes the barbarous names he judges to be later insertions by an Egyptian magical school and marks the rest with obeli, so most of the names in the ritual are not in the text read; the one long vowel chain that is printed survives because he holds it to belong to the ritual. And he renders one of the two prescribed sounds as puff rather than pop because he takes it to be connected with breath, which is a reading and not a translation."@en ;
    skos:note "Going behind Mead was done and lands on a third layer rather than on the papyrus. His source is Dieterich's edition, opened here in the second edition of 1910; Dieterich's source is Wessely's first reading of Paris papyrus 574. What Mead removed, Dieterich had already set in small type as the magicians' insertions, on his own judgement and not on any mark in the manuscript, and Mead's obeli are Dieterich's typography carried into English. The distinction between the liturgy and its magical redaction is therefore an editorial reconstruction from 1903, and every node in this file that speaks of what belongs to the rite is standing on it."@en ;
    skos:note "Dieterich says himself where he is certain that the received text already carried its own names, and the list is short: the vowel groups for the seven planets, the point where the gods are appeased, the opening of the disk, the appearance of the sun god, the protective cry at the gates, and the adjuration that the god remain in the operator's soul. Those places are the ones on which a reconstruction of the sounds may be attempted; the rest of the barbarous names he holds to be interchangeable stock that the magicians supplied from their own apparatus, so that even a perfect reading of the papyrus would not recover what the rite said there."@en ;
    skos:note "The name is a modern label twice over. Dieterich called the piece a liturgy in 1903; Mead objects that a liturgy has several participants while this is a rite for one person alone, and whether it is Mithraic at all is disputed in the scholarship. Neither question is settled here."@en ;
    skos:note "A second work of this corpus was opened on 2026-09-18 and its nodes stand in traditions/demotic-magical-papyri.ttl: the Papyrus of London and Leiden, read throughout in the translation of Griffith and Thompson of 1904. The registration named the demotic papyri from the start and nothing of them had been entered, so the tradition stood at pm:placesEntered on one piece of one Greek papyrus. The works are named as identifiers here from that date, which is what makes an unopened work of an opened tradition visible to a query at all (prima-materia#737)."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Papyri Graecae Magicae and Papyri Demoticae Magicae, including the ascent ritual at PGM IV.475-829" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:PgmIv, pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden, pmw:PgmXII, pmw:PgmXIII ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, in the translation of G. R. S. Mead, A Mithriac Ritual (London 1907)" .

#
# The procedure, which is the reason the corpus was opened
#

pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Drawing breath from the rays, three times as deeply as one can"@en, "Atem aus den Strahlen ziehen, dreimal so tief wie möglich"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first instruction of the rite: to take breath from the sun's rays, inhaling three times as deeply as the operator is able. Later stages prescribe the counter-movement — a prolonged bellowing like a horn-note, expelling the whole breath with pressure on the ribs, and a second bellowing with pressure on the belly to set the senses going all at once."@en ;
    skos:note "The most concrete bodily instruction in the whole of this graph's ascent material. It names the direction of the breath, its depth, its count and, at the exhalation, where the pressure is applied. Nothing in the ophite, hermetic or Jeu material comes near this, and it is the reason the register singled this corpus out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction and fifth and eighth utterances (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" .

pmc:PgmIvSublatio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Being raised aloft, hearing and seeing nothing mortal"@en, "Emporgehoben werden, nichts Sterbliches hören und sehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The effect announced for the breathing: the operator will see himself raised aloft and soaring towards the height, so that he seems to be in the midst of air; he will hear nothing, neither man nor beast, and will see nothing of the sights upon the earth in that same hour, but everything he sees will be immortal."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as what will happen and not as what may. The withdrawal of hearing and of earthly sight is given first and the vision second, which makes the sensory cut the condition rather than a side effect."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" .

pmc:PgmIvSpiritusYieldsSublatio a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The threefold indrawn breath raises the operator"@en, "Der dreifach eingezogene Atem hebt den Handelnden empor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:PgmIvSublatio ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    skos:note "The pairing is as tight as this graph holds anywhere: a named bodily act, a stated count, and an outcome that arrives in the same hour. What it still lacks is the other half — the text nowhere says what the operator sees or fails to see if it does not work."@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" .

pmc:PgmIvVocesSepties a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The vowel chain, uttered through seven times"@en, "Die Vokalkette, siebenmal durchgesprochen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one chain of vowels the translation prints in full, invoked as the deathless names innate with life which have not yet descended to mortal nature nor been made articulate by human tongue, with the instruction to utter them all with fire and spirit once to the end and then begin again, until the seven immortal gods of the cosmos are completed."@en ;
    skos:note "A dose and a stopping rule, which is rare. The chain is not said once but run through until a count of seven is reached, and the count is given by the gods rather than by the operator; the announced consequence is thunder and crashing in the surround, felt in the body at every crash."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim the words make about themselves is worth separating from their use. They are called names that have not yet come down to mortal nature and have never been made articulate by a human tongue — that is, the text asserts that what it prints cannot be what is meant. Any comparison of these syllables with the chains of the Jeu material or of the Pistis Sophia runs into that before it runs into the transliterations. See prima-materia#607."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, third utterance (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" .

#
# Two formulae, and what they are addressed to
#

pmc:PgmIvSilentiumAvertit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The word of silence turns the gods aside"@en, "Das Schweigenswort wendet die Götter ab"@de ;
    skos:definition "The exchange at the first threshold: the operator sees the gods gazing intently at him and bearing down upon him, lays his right finger on his lips and says Silence three times, calling silence the symbol of the living god beyond decay and asking it to protect him, then hisses long and puffs; whereupon the gods are said to gaze benignly and to bear down no longer, going about their proper order of doings."@en ;
    skos:note "A formula with an announced effect on a hostile power, and the effect is not passage but the withdrawal of pressure. The gods are not opened, moved or persuaded: they return to their own business. Beside the ophite gates, where a speech obtains a passage, this is a smaller and more precise claim."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first utterance (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" .

pmc:PgmIvIdentitasAstri a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "I am a star whose course is as your course"@en, "Ich bin ein Stern, dessen Bahn eure Bahn ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second utterance, said after silence and before the disk expands: that the speaker is a star whose course runs with the course of those addressed, shining anew out of the depth."@en ;
    skos:note "A bare statement of what the speaker is, addressed to powers, with no request attached. It is the same shape as the ophite speeches without their closing petition, and it stands in a corpus with a documented route to that one rather than at a distance from it — so it is a second occurrence and not a second witness."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is of kinship in motion and not in rank: the course is the same course. Whether that is what the ophite speaker means when he names the symbol he already bears is exactly the question this file cannot settle, and it is why the two are held apart."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, second utterance (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" .

#
# What is promised, and for how long
#

pmc:PgmIvImmortalitasHorae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Immortality for this same hour"@en, "Unsterblichkeit für eben diese Stunde"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed: that the operator, one out of so many tens of thousands, is transformed to immortality in this same hour; that his human soul-power subsists for a little while in purity and will afterwards be transmitted back to him; and, in the closing salutation, that being born again he passes away in being made great, and having been made great he dies."@en ;
    skos:note "The promise carries its own term, and the term is short. A rite that says the state lasts an hour and then returns the operator to his necessity is making a smaller claim than the ascents this graph holds elsewhere, and a more checkable one: it says the practitioner will be back."@en ;
    skos:note "The last sentence is not a flourish. Being made great and dying are put in sequence and the second follows from the first, which places the death inside the attainment rather than after it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, fifth and tenth utterances and invocatory logos (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" .

pmc:PgmIvSignaSuccessus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The ordered list of what will be seen"@en, "Die geordnete Liste dessen, was zu sehen sein wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sequence of announced sights, each given before the stage at which it is to occur: the disposition of the gods, some ascending and some descending; the pipe depending from the disk from which the wind comes; the gods bearing down and then desisting; a thunder-clap that startles; the disk expanding; a host of five-pointed stars filling the air; an infinite encircling with doors of fire fast closed; the doors thrown open; a god with locks of flame in a white tunic and scarlet mantle; seven virgins with serpent faces and golden sceptres; seven gods with the faces of black bulls; and a descending god holding the golden shoulder of the calf."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as one node and kept apart from anything that could be tested, for the reason SPEC §15 gives: these are signs of success, they are told to the operator in advance, and there is no base rate for a sight nobody measures without the telling. A corpus this rich in announced sights and this empty of stated failures is itself the finding."@en ;
    skos:note "The second half of that finding is withdrawn. This node said until 2026-09-09 that the rite carries no condition of failure at all, and that was a statement about the translation that had been read, not about the text: Mead breaks off with the liturgy, and the forfeiture clause stands in the magical appendix that follows it in the papyrus. What holds is the imbalance, and it is large — a dozen announced sights against one clause. What does not hold is the zero. See pmc:PgmIvTerInAnno and prima-materia#643."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first through eighth instructions and utterances (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" .

pmc:PgmIvTraditioClausa a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "A father may hand it on, and only to a son"@en, "Ein Vater darf es weitergeben, und nur an einen Sohn"@de ;
    skos:definition "The restriction stated in the opening prayer: that these are mysteries which a father alone may hand on, and that only to a son, an initiate worthy of the craft; the rite is for one person alone, who is to raise himself."@en ;
    skos:note "The same gate the project's criterion is aimed at, in a third transmission after the Ars Notoria and the ophite requirement of a curse before admission. The restriction here is narrower than either: not an order, not a school, but one person to one person."@en ;
    skos:note "It sits oddly beside the rite itself, which is expressly solitary and needs no officiant. What is withheld is therefore the text and not the performance, and a text that has been in print since nineteen hundred and seven is no longer withheld by it."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, the father's prayer (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" .

#
# What the edition carries and the translation did not
#

pmc:PgmIvSalutatioNominatim a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Fourteen powers, each greeted by its own name"@en, "Vierzehn Mächte, jede mit ihrem eigenen Namen begrüßt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The two ranks met after the gates open: seven maidens in linen with serpent faces and golden sceptres, called the Fates of heaven and guardians of the four pillars, and seven gods with the faces of black bulls, in linen aprons and with seven golden diadems, called the pole-rulers. Each rank is greeted first as a body and then member by member, and the text requires that each be addressed with its own name, given in order from first to seventh."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the nearest thing in the corpus to a gate formula that names its keeper. The requirement is explicit — each is to be greeted with the name proper to it — and the order is fixed, so what is transmitted is a sequence of fourteen names in two ranks, not an invocation that could be paraphrased. In the translation read first, all fourteen stand as obeli; they are in the edition."@en ;
    skos:note "Whether the names belong to the rite is the question the edition itself raises and does not close. Its editor holds that names must have stood in exactly these two places, which is a reconstruction from the shape of the passage rather than a mark in the manuscript, and he holds most other barbarous names in the piece to be interchangeable stock. One of the fourteen is read as Egyptian and as certainly belonging to the received text; that is the only one of them with an argument attached."@en ;
    skos:note "The greeting is not a request. The maidens are saluted as holy and good; the pole-rulers are told what they do — they turn the axle of heaven at a single command and hurl thunder, lightning, earthquakes and bolts at the tribes of the impious — and are then asked for health, bodily wholeness, keenness of hearing and sight, and freedom from disturbance for the good hours of this day. A passage is not asked for and no passage is granted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-723, the ninth and tenth utterances (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wünsch, Leipzig 1910)" .

pmc:PgmIvVocesSineLege a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The vowel groups of the seven follow no discoverable rule"@en, "Die Vokalgruppen der Sieben folgen keiner auffindbaren Regel"@de ;
    skos:definition "The state of the chain as the edition prints it: the seven vowels recur in groups through the invocation of the seven immortal gods of the cosmos, and the groups are not built by any pattern the editor could establish. He states that he put them to a mathematician and that no law of permutation was found in their arrangement."@en ;
    skos:note "The contrast is what makes this worth a node. Where a comparable arrangement is inscribed for the seven archangels of the planets in a theatre in Asia Minor, the rule is trivial and visible at a glance: the leading vowel of each row moves to the end of the next. The chain in this papyrus does not do that, and it does not do anything else that has been found. Either it is corrupt, or its rule is not positional, or there is none and the sequence is the point."@en ;
    skos:note "That bears directly on any comparison with the chains of the Books of Jeu and the Pistis Sophia. A comparison of two sequences is worth something if the sequences are generated; if one of them is unstructured, matching it against another matches accidents. See prima-materia#607."@en ;
    skos:note "The recitation carries a manner as well as a count: the whole is to be said with fire and with breath, run through to the end and begun again until the seven are completed. The prescribed non-verbal sounds sit in the same layer — a long hiss and a clucking of the lips — and are reported by the edition as an old stratum of address, attested for lightning in fifth-century Athens and for calling snakes, not as an invention of the magicians."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-723, the third utterance and the editor's apparatus to it (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wünsch, Leipzig 1910)" .

pmc:PgmIvTerInAnno a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Three times a year, and forfeited by whoever disregards the instruction"@en, "Dreimal im Jahr, und verwirkt für den, der die Anweisung überhört"@de ;
    skos:definition "The clause appended to the rite: this immortalization takes place three times in the year, and if anyone should wish, after the instruction has been given, to disregard it, it will no longer come about for him."@en ;
    skos:note "The one stated condition of failure in this piece, and it is not a sign to be watched for but a forfeiture. It does not say that the operator will see something different when it fails; it says that the thing will not happen at all, and it names the cause — disregard of the instruction after receiving it. That is checkable in the weak sense that the tradition can be held to it, and it is more than the announced sights offer, since none of them can fail to arrive in a way the text acknowledges."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands outside the liturgy proper, in the instruction for magical use that follows it in the papyrus, which is why a reading that stops at the end of the liturgy reports the rite as carrying no condition of failure. The same appendix later has the god himself change the frequency: no longer three times a year but once a month at the full moon, and with the ointment thrown into a river rather than used. A rule that the text alters within itself is a rule that somebody was following."@en ;
    skos:note "Two further constraints stand with it and are not conditions of success: for a second person to hear what is said, that person breathes with the operator for seven days and abstains from animal food and the bath; and the operator, when the god answers, is drained in soul and not in himself, while remembering unerringly what was said even if the oracle ran to ten thousand lines."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the appended instruction for magical use (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wünsch, Leipzig 1910)" .

#
# What the appendix prescribes, read in the edition on 2026-09-22
#
# The instruction for magical use runs from P 723 to P 834 and the translation
# first used stops before it. It is the operative half: it names the materials,
# the dates, the terms, what the operator will be like afterwards, and the one
# place in the whole piece where a test is stated with an outcome that could
# come out the other way. Dieterich prints it in Greek without a translation,
# so every node below is read off his Greek with his apparatus, and the place
# is given as the page and line of his text part, because his print carries the
# papyrus line numbers only as the range of the whole.
#

pmp:PgmIvConsecratioScarabaei a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The consecration of the scarab, begun when the moon is not to be seen"@en, "Die Weihe des Skarabaeus, begonnen wenn der Mond nicht zu sehen ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The preparation the appendix calls the teaching of the operation. A solar scarab with twelve rays is taken; at the seizure of the moon it is made to be thrown into a deep turquoise vessel together with the seed of the lotus pith, ground up with honey into a cake. The beetle comes to it and eats. It is then taken up and put into a glass vessel of the best rose oil, consecrated sand is strewn beneath, the vessel is set upon it, and the name is said over the vessel for seven days while the sun stands at the meridian. On the seventh day the beetle is taken out, dipped in myrrh, Mendesian wine and byssos, and laid away in a bean field in bloom; the ointment is then put away in purity, after a meal taken in company, for the immortalization."@en ;
    skos:note "A dated start, a term of seven days, a daily hour, and a seventh-day act. The start is fixed by a state of the moon and not by a day of a calendar, which is a harder condition than a date because it cannot be anticipated by counting from an arbitrary point. The hour is fixed by the sun standing at the meridian, so the recitation is tied twice over."@en ;
    skos:note "The materials are named by kind and not left open: the twelve-rayed solar beetle, the deep turquoise vessel, the seed of the lotus pith, honey, the best rose oil, consecrated sand, myrrh, Mendesian wine, byssos, and a bean field in bloom. SPEC section 3 parameter 1 provides for these as identifiers under a namespace of their own and no such identifier exists in the holding yet, so they stand here in the definition of the procedure. Nothing about their working is claimed by naming them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 17 line 7 to p. 18 line 5)" .

pmc:PgmIvScarabaeusMoritur a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "It comes and eats, and when it has eaten it dies at once"@en, "Er kommt und frisst, und wenn er gefressen hat, stirbt er sogleich"@de ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the appendix states for the first step of the consecration: the operator will at once see the beetle coming and eating, and when it has eaten, it dies immediately."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated observable inside a preparation, and it is of the plainest kind this holding carries: an animal either comes and eats and dies or it does not. The text puts it as what will happen and gives no alternative, so a run in which the beetle refuses the cake or lives is a run the wording does not provide for. That is not a sign of success told in advance for the operator to look out for; it is a step whose completion is visible, and the difference is that this one can be seen to fail."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 17 lines 11-12)" .

pmp:PgmIvUnctioOculorum a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Anointing another person's face with the juice of the herb and rose oil"@en, "Das Gesicht eines anderen mit dem Saft der Pflanze und Rosenoel salben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operation the appendix gives for showing the thing to someone else: the operator takes the juice of the herb called kentritis and anoints with it, together with rose oil, the face of whomever he wishes."@en ;
    skos:note "The only procedure in this piece performed on a second person's body rather than on the operator's own, and the only one whose announced result is a change in what that second person sees. The rite proper is expressly solitary; this is its outward-facing half."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 lines 6-8)" .

pmc:PgmIvVisusTelaugos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "He will see far-shiningly, so that you will marvel"@en, "Er wird weithin leuchtend sehen, so dass du dich wundern wirst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for the anointing of another's face: that person will see far-shiningly, the text says, so that the operator will marvel; and the author adds that he has found no greater operation than this in the world."@en ;
    skos:note "The superlative is the author's own and not a later editor's, and it is worth keeping because it sits next to the one condition of failure this piece states. A writer who calls something the greatest operation in the world has said what he thinks is at stake and has not said what would count against it."@en ;
    skos:note "What the promised seeing is remains unstated. The word says far-shining or bright to a distance and the text names no object, no duration and no way of telling the seeing from ordinary sight. Beside the ordered list of announced sights in the liturgy proper, this is the same kind of claim with the list removed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 lines 6-9)" .

pmc:PgmIvUnctioYieldsVisus a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The anointing of the face gives that person the far-shining sight"@en, "Die Salbung des Gesichts gibt jenem das weithin leuchtende Sehen"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:PgmIvUnctioOculorum ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:PgmIvVisusTelaugos ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    skos:note "Named act, named material, named recipient, announced effect, and no term and no condition of failure. The pairing is tight at the input end and open at the output end, which is the opposite arrangement from the beetle that dies when it has eaten."@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 lines 6-9)" .

pmc:PgmIvProbatioKentritidis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The way the herb is known: the feather whose barbs drop off at a touch"@en, "Wie die Pflanze zu erkennen ist: die Feder, deren Fahne bei der Beruehrung abfaellt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The recognition of the plant, given after its season and its habitat and a comparison with the upright vervain. The knowledge of it comes about thus: the black tip of an ibis feather is slackened and smeared with the juice, and at the mere touching the barbs fall off."@en ;
    skos:note "The one place in this piece where something is stated that could come out the other way. It is a test of a material with a definite outcome on an object neither the operator nor a client controls: either the barbs fall or they do not, and the text attaches the identity of the plant to that and to nothing else. The whole rest of the piece tells the operator what he will see; here it tells him what to do to find out."@en ;
    skos:note "It is a test of the ingredient and not of the operation, and that is where the interest lies. The same corpus carries a second of the kind, the assay of the white stone at pmc:PdmProbatioLapidis, and there too the test falls on what is bought rather than on what is done. Neither work states any comparable test for the apparition, the far-shining sight or the immortalization."@en ;
    skos:note "How the plant came to be known is reported as a revelation and the test is not: the lord pointed it out, and the plant was found in a named district at a named place beside another named plant. The finding is credited to the god; the way of checking a specimen is given as a procedure anybody could run."@en ;
    skos:note "The botanical identification is not part of the claim. The editor's friend, printed in the addenda of this same edition, argues the plant is a species of Ruscus and states that the name occurs nowhere else in the literature he knows. That is modern work and enters this holding only as pm:evidenceFrom at a protocol, never as a source here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 19 lines 9-11)" .

pmp:PgmIvPurificatioTriduana a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Keeping pure for three days beforehand"@en, "Drei Tage vorher rein bleiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The requirement standing before the meeting with the great god: having kept pure for three days, the operator is to come at dawn towards the east."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as a span and a direction and nothing else. What purity consists in is not said here; the appendix says elsewhere, of a second person, that he abstains from animal food and the bath, and that is the nearest the piece comes to a content."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the constituting of the great god (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 line 15)" .

pmp:PgmIvSystasisMagni a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The constituting of the great god at the conjunction in the Lion"@en, "Das Zusammentreten mit dem grossen Gott bei der Konjunktion im Loewen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operation by which the great god is brought into relation: carrying the aforesaid herb, at the conjunction that occurs in the Lion, the juice is taken and mixed with honey and myrrh; the eight-letter name is written with it on a leaf of the persea; the operator comes at dawn towards the east and licks off the leaf while showing it to the sun, and thus, the text says, the god will hear him perfectly. The consecration is to be begun at the new moon in the Lion, and the leaf is rolled up and put into the rose oil afterwards."@en ;
    skos:note "Two calendar conditions in one operation and they are not the same condition: the juice is taken at a conjunction in a named sign, and the consecration is begun at a new moon in the same sign. Both fix the time by where the luminaries stand and not by a date anyone keeps."@en ;
    skos:note "The stated result is that the god will hear perfectly. That is an improvement of a channel and not an event, so nothing about it could be seen not to have happened, and the piece supplies no sign for it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the constituting of the great god (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 lines 11-19)" .

pmc:PgmIvSystasisPraerequisitum a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The three days of purity stand before the constituting"@en, "Die drei Tage der Reinheit stehen vor dem Zusammentreten"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:PgmIvSystasisMagni ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:PgmIvPurificatioTriduana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, because the text gives the span and does not say what follows from skipping it. That is the whole of the difference: an operator who came on the second day would find nothing in the wording telling him what he had forfeited."@en ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the constituting of the great god (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 lines 15-16)" .

pmc:PgmIvUsusRepetitusEtCorrectio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Having used it often I marvelled, and the god told me to stop using the ointment"@en, "Vielfach habe ich es gebraucht und mich gewundert, und der Gott hiess mich die Salbe lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author's report of his own practice and of a change in his prescription. He says that having used the operation many times he marvelled exceedingly; and that the god said to him: use the ointment no longer, but throw it into a river, and operate wearing the great mystery of the scarab revived through the twenty-five living birds, and operate once a month at the full moon instead of three times in the year."@en ;
    skos:note "Two claims stand here and they are of different kinds. That the author used the operation repeatedly is a report of his own practice, which is the scarcest thing in a handbook of this sort and is what separates a copied recipe from a working one. That the prescription was then corrected is a revision of the rule, and the authority given for it is the same power the rule came from."@en ;
    skos:note "The shape of the correction is what makes it worth a node of its own rather than a remark at the clause it changes. A revision attributed to the revealing power cannot be wrong in the way a revision attributed to experience can: nothing the author saw is offered as the reason, no fault of the discarded ointment is named, and the new frequency is not derived from anything. So a text that does record repeated use records it without letting the use bear on the rule. The clause the correction overrides stands at pmc:PgmIvTerInAnno."@en ;
    skos:note "It also shows that somebody was following the rule. A frequency is not altered in a text nobody worked from, and the alteration is made in the first person by the writer of the handbook and not by a later hand set apart in the manuscript."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 19 lines 1-6)" .

pmc:PgmIvPetitioLibera a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Ask the god what you will, and he will give it to you"@en, "Bitte den Gott, was du willst, und er wird es dir geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The promise standing between the anointing and the constituting: ask of the god what you wish, and he will give it to you."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because its unconditionality is the finding and not a triviality. One sentence in this piece states a forfeiture and one sentence states a promise with no condition of any kind attached, and they are four pages apart in the same appendix. A reader who wants to know what the transmission thinks can be relied on has both."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the teaching of the operation (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 18 line 10)" .

pmc:PgmIvMemoriaInfallibilis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Drained in soul, and remembering the oracle without a slip"@en, "Erschoepft an der Seele, und das Orakel ohne Fehl erinnernd"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the appendix says of the operator once the god answers: he prophesies at once; the operator will be drained in soul and not in himself while he is being answered; the god speaks the oracle in verses and departs, and the operator stands there speechless. He will take all of it in of itself, and will then remember without deviation what the archangel said, even if the oracle ran to ten thousand verses."@en ;
    skos:note "A capacity stated with a quantity, which is rare enough to enter on that ground alone. Unerring recall of an utterance of any length is checkable in principle by anyone who has the utterance, and unlike the announced sights it is not a private appearance: the oracle comes in verses, and verses can be written down and compared."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the only place in the piece where the operator is said not to be in himself. The liturgy proper has him acting throughout — drawing breath, hissing, bellowing, greeting each power by name — and here, at the one moment when something is received rather than done, the agency is stated to be gone."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the close of the rite and the instruction for magical use (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 16 lines 1-7)" .

pmc:PgmIvExtasisSolitarii a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Alone, you speak what the god said as one out of himself"@en, "Allein sprichst du das Wort des Gottes wie ein Ausser-sich-Seiender"@de ;
    skos:definition "The clause for the operator working without a companion: if you are alone and undertake the things spoken by the god, you speak them as one in ecstasy, uttering them as one possessed."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause distinguishes the solitary run from the run with a second person and says that the difference falls on the manner of speaking. With a companion the words are said over his head so low that he cannot hear them; alone they are said in ecstasy. The same words, and the transmission specifies a different delivery for each case."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the instruction for magical use (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 16 lines 11-12)" .

pmc:PgmIvTraditioSubmissa a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Judge him worthy, then say it over his head so that he cannot hear it"@en, "Halte ihn fuer wuerdig, dann sprich es ueber seinem Kopf, damit er es nicht hoert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The procedure for admitting a second person. The operator is first to judge whether that man is safely worthy, using the method by which he would be judged himself in the immortalization; he then puts the first utterance to him, whose opening is given; and the remainder he speaks as an initiate over the man's head in a toneless voice, so that he may not hear it, anointing his face with the mystery."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission restricts its own passing on inside the operative text, and here the restriction is put into the performance rather than into a rule about it. The candidate undergoes the rite and does not receive the words: he is anointed, the greater part is recited above him inaudibly, and what he is given is the effect and not the text."@en ;
    skos:note "It sits with pmc:PgmIvTraditioClausa, which restricts the handing on to one person by one person, and it is the sharper of the two, because a rule of secrecy can be broken by a copyist and this cannot. A reader of the papyrus has the words; the man who was initiated by its owner did not."@en ;
    skos:note "The judgement of worthiness is left to the operator with no criterion attached beyond the words safely, as a man, and a back-reference to the way he would be judged himself. Where a criterion is missing the decision falls to whoever holds the text, which is the arrangement this project's own criterion is aimed at."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:PgmIvSpiritusTractus ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the instruction for magical use (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 16 line 13 to p. 17 line 4)" .

pmp:PgmIvPhylakteriaDuo a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Two amulets, one on the skin of a black sheep and one on that of a white"@en, "Zwei Amulette, eines auf der Haut eines schwarzen und eines auf der eines weissen Schafs"@de ;
    skos:definition "The making of the amulets the rite has the operator kiss at two of its stages. The right one is written on the membrane of a black sheep with myrrh-black ink, and is bound round with sinews of the same animal; the left one on the membrane of a white sheep, and is used in the same way. The left is stated to be for those well disposed."@en ;
    skos:note "The liturgy proper has the operator kiss the amulets twice and says nothing of where they come from; the appendix supplies the material and the writing. Colour is made to carry the difference between the two, and the binding is required to come from the same animal as the skin."@en ;
    skos:note "The text that is written on them is given as four Homeric hexameters, on which see pmc:PgmIvVersusHomerici. The right amulet is not given a purpose in the text as it stands, and the editor notes that the formula for the left one appears to be missing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the amulets (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 20 lines 3-8)" .

pmc:PgmIvVersusHomerici a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "Verses of Homer used as charms, one of them under no stated purpose"@en, "Homerverse als Zauber gebraucht, einer davon ohne genannten Zweck"@de ;
    skos:definition "The practice the appendix closes with: single hexameters of the Iliad are set down as operative formulae, each under a heading naming what it is for. Four stand with the amulets; one stands under the heading common, which names no effect; two more carry effects and are entered separately. The editor notes that the same verses stand in the papyrus immediately before the immortalization as well as after it, so that the rite is enclosed by them on both sides."@en ;
    skos:note "The heading common is entered as it stands and not filled in. A heading that names no effect is not a gap in the reading: it says that the transmission had a use for a verse that it did not need to specify, and a reader who supplied a purpose for it would be supplying the only thing that could have been tested."@en ;
    skos:note "That verses of an epic are used at all is the finding beside the voces magicae. Where the barbarous names assert of themselves that they have never been uttered by a human tongue, these lines were the most widely known poetry in the language, and the same appendix puts them to work in the same way."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the amulets and the closing charms (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 20 lines 9-12 and p. 21 lines 3-4)" .

pmc:PgmIvThymokatochon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "A restrainer of anger, being one verse of Homer"@en, "Ein Zornbaendiger, naemlich ein Homervers"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for a single hexameter set under the heading restrainer of anger: the line in which the hearer is asked whether he will dare to lift the monstrous spear against Zeus."@en ;
    skos:note "A recipe of the shortest possible kind — one heading, one line, nothing else — and it is a capacity like any other. No dose, no time, no condition, no sign, and no statement of what would show the anger unrestrained."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the closing charms (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 21 lines 5-6)" .

pmc:PgmIvProsPhilous a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "For friends, being one verse of Homer"@en, "An die Freunde, naemlich ein Homervers"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed for a single hexameter set under the heading towards friends: the line asking that the enemy not be given cause to rejoice over them."@en ;
    skos:note "The same shape as the restrainer of anger and the same silence, and the two together are what a handbook of this kind looks like when it is not writing a liturgy: a purpose, a line, and no account of either."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GreekMagicalPapyri ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the closing charms (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 21 lines 7-8)" .

#
# Where the rite stands on the axes of awareness
#

pmc:PgmIvSublatioIsWithdrawn a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The raising is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en, "Die Erhebung liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:PgmIvSublatio ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situationGround "The source states the withdrawal before the vision: in that hour the operator hears nothing, neither man nor beast, and sees nothing of the things upon the earth. That is the criterion this coordinate names, and the source supplies it as an instruction about what will be the case and not as a description of a mood. The operator is awake throughout and is acting — drawing breath, speaking, pressing his side — so none of the sleep or dream values applies."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" .

pmc:PgmIvSublatioIsDisplaced a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "The raising is a displaced locus"@en, "Die Erhebung ist ein verlagerter Ort"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:PgmIvSublatio ;
    pm:axisValue pm:displacedLocus ;
    pm:situationGround "The source has the operator see himself lifted and passing upward, so that he seems to be in the midst of the air, and later has him stand among the rays and meet ranks of powers coming out of a depth. The awareness is put somewhere the body is not, which is the criterion for this value. Whether anything of the sort occurs is a different question and not this one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.475-829, first instruction (trans. G. R. S. Mead, London 1907)" .

pmc:PgmIvOraculumIsCeded a pm:Situating ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PgmIv ;
    rdfs:label "At the answering the agency is ceded"@en, "Beim Antworten ist das Handeln abgegeben"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:PgmIvMemoriaInfallibilis ;
    pm:axisValue pm:cededAgency ;
    pm:situationGround "The appendix states of this one moment that the operator is drained in soul and not in himself while he is being answered, and that he stands there speechless; the taking in of the oracle is said to happen of itself. Everywhere else in the piece the source has him act, so the placement is of the answering and not of the rite."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "PGM IV.723-834, the close of the rite (ed. A. Dieterich, Eine Mithrasliturgie, 2nd ed. by R. Wuensch, Leipzig 1910, p. 16 lines 1-7)" .

#
# The works of this corpus as identifiers
#

pmw:PgmIv a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "PGM IV, the great magical papyrus of Paris (Bibliotheque nationale suppl. gr. 574)"@en, "PGM IV, der grosse Zauberpapyrus von Paris (Bibliotheque nationale suppl. gr. 574)"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened at one piece, the ascent ritual at lines 475 to 829, in two layers: the English of Mead of 1907 and the edition of Dieterich in the second edition of 1910. The codex runs to thirty-six pages and the rest of it is unopened."@en .

pmw:PapyrusLondonLeiden a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Papyrus of London and Leiden (PDM xiv)"@en, "Papyrus von London und Leiden (PDM xiv)"@de ;
    skos:note "One manuscript in two museums, of the third century of this era, twenty-nine columns on the recto and thirty-three short columns on the verso. Opened on 2026-09-18 in the edition of Griffith and Thompson, volume I of 1904, which prints transliteration, translation and notes; the translation was read throughout and the transliteration was not used. The editors state that the language makes it difficult to believe that any part of the work in its present redaction is more than a century or two older than the papyrus itself."@en ;
    skos:note "What the reading covered and what it left: the whole of the translation of the recto, columns I to XXIX, and of the verso, columns I to XXXIII, was read at the wording. Not read: the transliteration on the facing pages and the philological notes beneath both, except where a note bears on a passage entered; the introduction, apart from its account of the manuscript and its table of contents; and the glossary volume of 1909, which was not obtained. The digitised copy used is an optical transcription and its rendering of the transliterated demotic is not trustworthy at the level of single letters, which is why no node in this holding reproduces a demotic form from it."@en ;
    skos:note "The nodes read off it stand in traditions/demotic-magical-papyri.ttl."@en .

pmw:PgmXII a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "PGM XII / PDM xii, the Leiden papyrus J 384"@en, "PGM XII / PDM xii, der Leidener Papyrus J 384"@de ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened. It is the second of the bilingual handbooks of the same find and stands to the Papyrus of London and Leiden as its nearest neighbour; a run that wants a second demotic witness for anything entered from PDM xiv goes here first."@en .

pmw:PgmXIII a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "PGM XIII, the Leiden papyrus J 395"@en, "PGM XIII, der Leidener Papyrus J 395"@de ;
    skos:note "Named here as a work of the corpus and unopened."@en .
